<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · movement</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catharism</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Albigensians, Cathars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cathars were a Christian dualist movement of the 12th and 13th centuries, strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, who believed the material world was the creation of an evil principle. Led by ascetic 'perfects', they rejected the sacraments and hierarchy of the Catholic Church. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albigensian-crusade">Albigensian Crusade</a> against them in 1209, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-inquisition">Inquisition</a> had largely eliminated them by the early 14th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catharism.jpg" alt="Catharism" width="320" height="496" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pedro Berruguete · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berruguete_ordeal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
